  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nWSW mid/upper flow persists over the Central Plains this afternoon \nwith a tight pressure gradient and breezy southerly flow across the \nregion. A rogue shower or thunderstorm may be possible late this \nafternoon and evening (through around 10 PM) along the prefrontal \ntrough axis or along the cold front across central KS where low \nlevel mass convergence will allow sfc dewpoints to pool. A capping \ninversion should preclude more widespread development. We could also \nsee some patchy fog development along and in the wake of the frontal \nboundary across parts of central KS late tonight and into the \nmorning hours on Thu where light ENE low winds are progged and \nboundary layer moisture may not be scoured out.\n\nAs we move into Thu, an uncapped airmass and abundant moisture in \nthe pbl could allow for an afternoon shower or storm but with some \nmid-lvl height rises under the influence of ridging, confidence \nremains rather low. The remnant frontal boundary could also provide \na focus and this boundary is expected to be situated along I-70 \ncorridor where some low pops were maintained late in the period and \ninto the evening hours as we see some enhancement of the LLJ nosing \ninto mainly northern KS.\n\nA more robust shortwave trough is progged to lift out of the \nSouthern High Plains as we move into the day on Friday. This should \nbring a period of more widespread shower with embedded thunderstorm \nactivity to the area as we move into the late morning/early \nafternoon hours with activity lingering into Friday night and \nSaturday as the negatively trough lifts over the area. We continue \nto see some timing differences but there may be a break in the \naction as we move into Sunday with shortwave ridging returning to \nthe area.\n\nThis break will be short-lived as another more robust mid/upper \ntrough is progged to emerge from the Rockies early next week \nbringing increasing chances for showers and thunderstorms once \nagain. As the previous shift noted, this system could bring \nunsettled weather through the middle of the week with strong winds, \nheavy rain and some potential for strong or severe storms as well. \nStay tuned as we refine the details in the coming days.",
